From 497e360b0614c8eb0536a65f6ba263f479852d93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 08:00:44 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Aurora: Wind Waker HD's monotone at the game's grey composite
 (aurora_set_monotone_hd)

Frozen Hyrule is sepia in Wind Waker HD: while the game's mMonotone is set it
draws its DOF pass with its monotone presets, then the player's lists (Link
and his things), then bloom over all of it. The GameCube's grey is one quad
in drawDepth that lays an I8 copy of the frame back over the scene, before
the player's lists.

With the host's monotone on, the I8 copy to mFrameBufferTex that follows the
depth copy marks the point: its quad is left out, and at the start of the
pass after it (its sun map already has that pass's casters, Link's) the
scene's effects run (no far blur, as HD; no bloom) and then HD's pass: a 2x2
box of the scene at half size, its grey by HD's weights, saturate of the way,
times HD's multiply, laid over the scene by clamp(level x clamp((distance -
start) / (end - start))), in linear light, as pixel shader 40716 in the
Hyroom capture. After the pass's last perspective draw, what was drawn there
writing depth and lit by no HD light of its own (an arrow, a bomb) gets the
lighting on a copy, laid back where it is nearer than the depth at the
point, then bloom goes over everything. No other effect goes in before the
point in such a frame (a copy laid back earlier is the game's motion blur,
under the sepia). The I8 copy keeps its colour beside the intensity, as HD's
does, for what samples it (the heat haze, the motion blur). The parameters
ride on the frame's passes, so each in-between frame draws the same.

Off by default; single-sampled only (aurora_monotone_hd_supported());
DOL_AURORA_MONOTONE_HD=0 turns it off. DOL_AURORA_PASS_TRACE takes a frame
number and prints each pass's monotone marks.

The heat haze in a monotone frame (Xboss0, the boss replays in sepia) takes
the frame as it is with HD's monotone in. Its particles sample the grey
composite's copy, which keeps the colour for HD's monotone, and they are
drawn after the monotone: each showed its quad of the unmonotoned colours
over the sepia, square-edged, across the lava.

The copy is made again from the frame after the monotone into a second
texture, which the frame's draws after the copy sample (the game's grey
quad is still left out). The copy itself goes back in place at the frame's
end, so the next frame's motion blur, laid back under the monotone, takes
the colour as before.
